c0da6e45d18abbf42460077f.content:
The order-dependent flakiness and the subsequent `NullPointerException` are caused by the shared static `Mockery` context and the shared static `NMEAMessageHandler` instance. When the polluter test runs first, it leaves the `NMEAMessageHandler` in a modified state (buffering a fragmented message) and corrupts the expectations of the static `JUnit4Mockery`. When the victim test runs, the static mock fails to capture the invocation properly, leading to a `null` captured object and an NPE upon calling `getMessageType()`.

To fix this deterministically without altering the class-level fixtures, we can instantiate a local `Mockery`, mock, and `NMEAMessageHandler` within each test method. This completely isolates the tests from each other and ensures JMock's expectations are evaluated independently per test.

//<victim start>
    @Test
    public void canHandleFragmentedMessageReceived() {
        Mockery localContext = new JUnit4Mockery();
        Consumer<AISMessage> localHandler = (Consumer<AISMessage>) localContext.mock(Consumer.class);
        NMEAMessageHandler localReceiver = new NMEAMessageHandler("TEST", localHandler);

        NMEAMessage fragmentedNMEAMessage1 = NMEAMessage.fromString("!AIVDM,2,1,3,B,55DA><02=6wpPuID000qTf059@DlU<00000000171lMDD4q20LmDp3hB,0*27");
        NMEAMessage fragmentedNMEAMessage2 = NMEAMessage.fromString("!AIVDM,2,2,3,B,p=Mh00000000000,2*4C");

        final ArgumentCaptor<AISMessage> aisMessage = new ArgumentCaptor<>();

        localContext.checking(new Expectations() {{
            oneOf(localHandler).accept(with(aisMessage.getMatcher()));
        }});

        localReceiver.accept(fragmentedNMEAMessage1);
        localReceiver.accept(fragmentedNMEAMessage2);

        localContext.assertIsSatisfied();
        assertEquals(AISMessageType.ShipAndVoyageRelatedData, aisMessage.getCapturedObject().getMessageType());
    }
//<victim end>

//<polluter start>
